            ("The brain inside the wall", "The wall is not only about legs. The brain runs almost exclusively on glucose and, when blood sugar drops, it precautionarily reduces the signal sent to the muscles: the so-called central governor. The result is a heaviness and confusion that seems to come from nowhere. That is why many marathoners remember the wall as a mental moment even before a physical one."),
            ("Training fat metabolism", "The body has two tanks: about 2,000 calories of glycogen and tens of thousands stored as fat. The problem is that fat burns slowly. Long slow runs teach muscles to use more of it, sparing glycogen; some athletes even train fasted for this reason. Well-trained runners push the wall beyond the finish line because they spend their sugars frugally."),
            ("Race-day strategy", "Three rules keep almost everyone away from the wall. Start at the planned pace and not faster, because every second gained early costs precious glycogen. Take carbohydrates during the race, 30-60 grams per hour from gels and drinks, starting early rather than when the crisis has begun. And reach the start line with full stores thanks to two or three days of carbohydrate-rich meals."),
